<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wolter von Plettenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1450–<a href="/years/1535">1535</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wolter von Plettenberg was Master of the Livonian Order from 1494 to 1535 and the leading figure of the Livonian Confederation. His victory over Muscovite forces at Smolino in 1502 secured half a century of peace on the eastern frontier. He tolerated the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in Riga and the other towns while keeping the Order Catholic, and he is often regarded as the Order's most capable master.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wolter-von-plettenberg.jpg" alt="Wolter von Plettenberg" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wolter_von_Plettenberg_(1450-1535).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Plettenberg was born at Meyerich Castle in Welver, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, the first child of Berthold von Plettenberg and Gosteke Lappe. He went to the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narva">Narva</a> at ten and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> in Riga at about fourteen. He was the Order's trade counsellor in 1481 and commander of Rositten Castle from 1482 to 1488, and in 1489 Grand Master Johann Freytag von Loringhoven made him marshal, the military leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. On 30 March 1491 he defeated the Archbishopric of Riga and the city of Riga at Neuermühlen, establishing the Order as the protector of Livonia. He was elected master on 7 July 1494.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> closed the Hanseatic office in Novgorod in 1494, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a> drifted towards war. Plettenberg allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-jagiellon">Alexander Jagiellon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Wenden</a> of 1500 and won at the Siritsa River in August 1501 against a Russian army about twice the size of his own, but dysentery, a defeat at Helmet and a failed siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> followed. At Lake Smolina on 13 September 1502 his 5,000 men beat about 12,000 Russians. The peace of 1503 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> was renewed in 1509, 1521 and 1531, and he kept Livonia neutral between Poland-Lithuania and Muscovy.</p><p>In 1525 he took northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>, including Tallinn, under his direct rule from the Prussian branch of the Order. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, he allowed Lutheran services from 1522, so that it was introduced without bloodshed, but he himself remained Catholic and in 1525-1526 refused to secularise the Order, despite pressure from Riga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. He was made a Prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1526, confirmed by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1530, and named Hermann von Brüggenei his successor in 1532.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died at Wenden Castle on 28 February 1535, aged about 85; legend says he died in full armour in an armchair by the fire. He is buried in Cēsis at St. John's church. Baltic German and Estonian historians have traditionally considered him one of the most capable and successful leaders in Livonian history. His bust stands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walhalla">Walhalla memorial</a> near Regensburg, and reliefs of about 1515 show him with the Madonna at the gateway of Riga Castle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wolter von Plettenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The town contributed money and ships to the defence of Livonia, including the campaigns against Muscovy led by Master <b>Wolter von Plettenberg</b> in the early sixteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Livonian Order's master, especially <b>Wolter von Plettenberg</b>, was the leading military figure, and the Hanseatic towns of Riga, Reval and Dorpat had great weight.</blockquote><a href="/state/livonian-confederation">Livonian Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>Master <b>Wolter von Plettenberg</b> defeated Muscovy at Smolina in 1502, but the Livonian War from 1558 was a disaster, with defeat at Ergeme in 1560.</blockquote><a href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Haus Meyerich, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Cēsis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.61,7.95&amp;year=1505" aria-label="Haus Meyerich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Wolter von Plettenberg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5421 5690h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5421 5690h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.61,7.95&amp;year=1505">What was here in 1505? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
